    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/qara-yusuf"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qara-yusuf.jpg" alt="Qara Yusuf" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/qara-yusuf">Qara Yusuf</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qara_Yusuf_enthroned,_15th_century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">15th century artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Qara Qoyunlu, the Black Sheep Turkomans, were a Turkoman, Muslim and culturally Persianate dynasty whose name most likely came from the black sheep painted on their flags, or from the colour of their flocks. The ruling family descended from the Baharlu of the Yıwa tribe of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz Turks</a>, who by the fourteenth century held lands north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-van">Lake Van</a> and around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Upper Mesopotamia</a>. Around them gathered the Saadlu, Karamanlu, Alpaut, Agacheri, Dukharlu, Jagirlu and Hajilu tribes, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhchivan">Nakhchivan</a> and Ganja to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahramanmaras">Maraş</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzurum">Erzurum</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a>; their own traditions say they came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> with the Mongol conquest.</p><p>In the fourteenth century they lived in the shadow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalayirid-sultanate">Jalayirid Sultanate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>. In 1366 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaykh-uways">Shaykh Uways</a> Jalayir defeated their leader Birdi Khwaja at Mosul and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayram-khwaja">Bayram Khwaja</a> at the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mus">Mush</a>, and from about 1375 the Qara Qoyunlu were Jalayirid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> ruling from Mosul. Qara Mahammad invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardin">Mardin</a> in 1384 and took the submission of its Artuqid ruler. Then came <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>: in 1400 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurids</a> defeated the Qara Qoyunlu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-yusuf">Qara Yusuf</a> fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> an-Nasir Faraj, unwilling to anger Timur, imprisoned him with the Jalayirid sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-jalayir">Ahmad Jalayir</a>. In prison the two agreed that Ahmad should keep Baghdad and Qara Yusuf have <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, Ahmad adopted Qara Yusuf's son Pirbudag, and both were released on Timur's death in 1405.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/etchmiadzin-cathedral"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/etchmiadzin-cathedral.jpg" alt="Etchmiadzin Cathedral" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/etchmiadzin-cathedral">Etchmiadzin Cathedral</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Etchmiadzin_2025-3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yerevantsi, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Qara Qoyunlu were a loose confederation of pastoral tribes whose wealth lay in flocks; mutton was the staple of their diet. Their rule over settled peoples rested on the taxes and labour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> in the towns and plains they held from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>. The Armenian historian Tovma Metsopetsi records heavy taxes on the Armenians after the conquest of 1410 but relatively peaceful early years in which some towns were rebuilt. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-iskander">Qara Iskander</a>, however, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> was made a “desert” and subjected to “devastation and plunder, to slaughter, and captivity”; many Armenians were taken captive and sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in his wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurids</a>, Jahan Shah's forces sacked the Tatev monastery in Syunik, and many left the land.</p><p>Jahan Shah also allotted land to Armenian lords, rebuilt churches and in 1441 approved the move of the Catholicos of the Armenian Apostolic Church to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etchmiadzin-cathedral">Etchmiadzin Cathedral</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> suffered repeated raids from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-yusuf">Qara Yusuf</a>'s attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhaltsikhe">Akhaltsikhe</a> in 1416 onward. The cities kept their crafts: Tabriz was a centre of artists and calligraphers, a group of whom Baysunghur carried off to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> in 1421. The court was Persianate, and Qara Qoyunlu rule speeded the process by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> became ethnically and linguistically Turkic. Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread in their lands through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaykh-junayd">Shaykh Junayd</a> of the Safavid order and the Musha'sha' in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzistan</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/shah-rukh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shah-rukh.jpg" alt="Shah Rukh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/shah-rukh">Shah Rukh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Contemporary_portrait_of_Shah_Rukh,_painted_in_his_lifetime_in_1435-36,_by_commission_of_his_son_Ibrahim_Sultan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1435-1436 artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Qara Qoyunlu fought as Turkoman tribal cavalry. Having lost to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalayirid-sultanate">Jalayirids</a> in 1366 and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> in 1400, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-yusuf">Qara Yusuf</a> returned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1405, forced Timur's governor of Van to submit and captured the viceroy Altamış. He defeated the Timurid prince Abu Bakr at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhchivan">Nakhchivan</a> on 14 October 1406 and reoccupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>; when Abu Bakr and his father Miran Shah tried to recover <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, he crushed them at the Battle of Sardrud on 20 April 1408, where Miran Shah was killed. In 1409 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardin">Mardin</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artuqid-dynasty">Artuqids</a>' last stronghold; in 1410 he conquered Tabriz, had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaykh-uways">Shaykh Uways</a> Jalayir executed and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> fell under his control.</p><p>Timurid power did not accept the loss. After Qara Yusuf's death in 1420 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-rukh">Shah Rukh</a> beat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-iskander">Qara Iskander</a> in 1420–21 and briefly occupied Tabriz, beat him again in 1429, and in a third expedition of 1434–35 installed Iskander's brother Jahan Shah as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. Jahan Shah, independent after Shah Rukh's death in 1447, seized Saveh, Qum, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazd">Yazd</a> in 1452–1453, and in the summer of 1458 held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> for some months until his son Hasan Ali's revolt and Abu Said's march on Tabriz forced him back. Princes and beys kept their own trained and salaried soldiers, the nökers. The last war was with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a>: Jahan Shah's attempt to take Diyarbakır from them in 1466 ended in catastrophe, and he died at the Battle of Chapakchur in 1467.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-minorsky"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vladimir-minorsky.jpg" alt="Vladimir Minorsky" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-minorsky">Vladimir Minorsky</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vladimir_Minorsky.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Georges Chevalier, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Qara Qoyunlu state was organised on the model of its predecessors, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalayirid-sultanate">Jalayirids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanids</a>. Jahan Shah, crowned on 19 April 1438 with the epithet Muzaffar al-Din, took the titles sultan and khan on becoming independent in 1447 and styled himself Padishah-i <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and King of Kings of Iran. The court used Persian for diplomacy, poetry and administration: letters to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurids</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> were written in Persian and those to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> in Arabic. Provinces were governed by şehzades, princes of the house, and by beys, each with a smaller divan, the beys' posts generally passing from father to son; in the cities officials called darugha handled finance and administration and held political power.</p><p>The dynasty's weakness was its own family. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-yusuf">Qara Yusuf</a>'s death in 1420 set his descendants fighting, and the Timurids exploited the quarrel to replace Iskander with Jahan Shah. Jahan Shah's sons rebelled in turn: Hasan Ali, imprisoned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maku">Maku</a>, rose in 1458; Pir Budaq, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>, sought independence, was spared at his mother's request and moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, rebelled again and was defeated in 1464 and executed on his father's orders. On religion the evidence is mixed: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-minorsky">Vladimir Minorsky</a> saw the Qara Qoyunlu adopting Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> as a unifying ideology for a loose tribal confederation, forerunners of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a>, yet their coins named the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>, and Bosworth finds “no strong evidence” of Shia sympathies.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/quli-qutb-mulk"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quli-qutb-mulk.jpg" alt="Quli Qutb Mulk" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/quli-qutb-mulk">Quli Qutb Mulk</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portret_van_Bara_Malik_hij_is_eerst_vizier_geweest_van_de_sultan_die_over_de_stad_Bidar_regeerde_later_werd_hij_heerser_van_Golconda_en_heeft_hij_Golconda_gesticht,_RP-T-00-3186-19A.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rijksmuseum, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Qara Qoyunlu's lasting work is in brick and tile. Jahan Shah commissioned the northern iwan of the Darb-e <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> in 1453, two years after taking the city, “a masterpiece of tile decoration”; he restored the eastern iwan of the Jameh Mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazd">Yazd</a> in 1457, and a foundation of his wife built the Blue Mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, finished in 1465, whose “unrivalled” underglaze tiles and mosaics in cobalt blue with incised gold and white covered walls, exterior and dome. This <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> tilework travelled: the blue-and-white tiles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and of Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bursa">Bursa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Edirne</a> are thought to be the work of itinerant artists from Tabriz, and “Abdallah of Tabriz” signed the tiles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dome-of-the-rock">Dome of the Rock</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Süleyman in 1545–1552.</p><p>In the arts of the book Pir Budaq, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, sponsored innovations in nasta'liq <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a> and finely illustrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>. Jahan Shah wrote poetry in Azerbaijani and Persian, and Abu Bakr Tehrani's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kitab-i-diyarbakriyya">Kitab-i Diyarbakriyya</a> chronicled the dynasty in Persian. Politically the dynasty broke the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalayirid-sultanate">Jalayirids</a>, kept western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> out of Timurid hands and, by Minorsky's account, prepared the Shia ground the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> built on; its people's language became Azerbaijani. Its descendants ruled far away: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quli-qutb-mulk">Sultan Quli</a>, a fourth-generation descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-iskander">Qara Iskander</a>, founded the Qutb Shahi Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a> in 1518, and Bairam Khan of the Baharlu commanded the Mughal army.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan.jpg" alt="Uzun Hasan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uzun_Hasan_on_horse,_1460s%E2%80%931470s,_Aq_Qoyunlu_ruler_Uzun_Hasa_hunting_(detail).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1467-1473 artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Jahan Shah won much territory, but his reign was troubled by rebellious sons and by the near-autonomous rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, whom he expelled in 1464. The eastern campaigns had shown the limits of Turkoman power: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> could be held only for months in 1458, and peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurids</a> was sealed by marrying a daughter to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Muhammad bin Baysonqor. The deadlier rival stood in the west. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a>, the White Sheep Turkomans of Diyarbakır, were the Qara Qoyunlu's old enemies and, probably out of that enmity, rejected the heterodox religious currents their rivals tolerated. In 1466 Jahan Shah attempted to take Diyarbakır from them; the campaign ended in catastrophic failure, and in 1467 he was killed at the Battle of Chapakchur.</p><p>His death brought the collapse of Qara Qoyunlu control across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>. Hasan Ali, the son who had twice rebelled against him, came to power but was killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a>, and by 1468 the Aq Qoyunlu under Uzun Hasan (1452–1478), at the height of their power, had defeated the Qara Qoyunlu and conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. What survived were monuments and people: the mausoleum of the Blue Mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> went on being built under the Aq Qoyunlu into the 1480s, the Tabriz school of tilework worked for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> into the mid-sixteenth century, the Armenian Catholicosate stayed at Etchmiadzin, and the Baharlu carried the name east to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a> and the Mughal court.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Qara Qoyunlu, a Turkoman confederation from the lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-van">Lake Van</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, threw off Jalayirid overlordship under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-yusuf">Qara Yusuf</a> in 1410, held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurids</a>, and under Jahan Shah ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> and briefly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, before Jahan Shah's death at Chapakchur in 1467 handed their lands to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a>.</em></p>

